.TH SVR3 4 "2026-10-05" "Substrate" "Kernel Interfaces"
.SH NAME
svr3 \- UNIX System V Release 3 execution personality and COFF loader
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B SVR3
personality
.RB ( PERS_SVR3 )
runs the i386 COFF programs of UNIX System V/386 Release 3 and of the
systems built on it, INTERACTIVE UNIX among them.  It is rooted at
.IR /perso/svr3 ,
which is where a program's absolute paths and its shared libraries are
looked for first.
.SS Executable format
Every i386 COFF executable
.RB ( f_magic
0x14c, the
.B F_EXEC
flag, a
.B ZMAGIC
optional header) is given this personality.  Such a file is laid out to be
paged straight from disk and is loaded as it lies:
.B .text
at the address that is its offset in the file, just past the headers in
the first page;
.B .data
4 MiB above, at the same offset within its page that it has in the file;
.B .bss
after it.  Nothing is relocated.  Sections marked
.B NOLOAD
or
.B DSECT
are left out.  The stack is at the top of user space and starts with
.IR argc ,
.IR argv[] ,
a null pointer,
.IR envp[] ,
and a null pointer.
.SS Shared libraries
Most programs are linked against a static shared library,
.IR /shlib/libc_s :
a COFF file of its own (optional-header magic 0443) whose sections are at
fixed addresses from 0xa0000000 up.  A program lists the libraries it needs
in a
.B .lib
section, and each is mapped at its addresses when the program is loaded.
The mappings are private to the process.  A program whose library cannot
be opened is not run.
.SS System call interface
A system call is
.BR "lcall $7,$0" ,
as in
.BR svr4 (4)
and the 32-bit half of
.BR xenix (4):
the number in
.BR EAX ,
arguments on the stack, carry for an error, a second result in
.BR EDX .
Release 4 kept Release 3's calls as they were, so the calls are those
.BR svr4 (4)
lists, served by the same code; what differs is that
.B utssys
reports Release 3.2 and that a signal handler is entered with the signal
number alone.  A call that is not implemented fails with
.BR EINVAL .
.PP
.BR ulimit (2)
command 3, the highest break the process may have, answers the current
break and 16 MiB: programs such as
.BR sort (1)
take all of the difference for working memory.
.SH DEBUGGING
.B debug=perso:svr3:syscall
on the kernel command line traces every call;
.B debug=exec:coff
reports the libraries a program loads and where it is entered.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /perso/svr3
The personality's root.
.I tools/svr3/build-image.sh
builds an image of it from AT&T's Release 3 media, and
.I tools/svr3/build-image-isc.sh
from INTERACTIVE UNIX's; neither set is part of substrate.
.TP
.I sys/exec/formats/coff.c
The loader.
.TP
.I sys/exec/perso/perso_svr3.c
The personality.
.TP
.I sys/exec/perso/svr4/svr4_calls.c
Its calls, with Release 4's.
.TP
.I sys/exec/perso/perso_sysv386.c
The entry convention and the calls common to it and Xenix/386.
.SH KNOWN LIMITATIONS
.IP \(bu 2
Only
.B ZMAGIC
executables are loaded;
.B OMAGIC
and
.B NMAGIC
ones are refused.
.IP \(bu
A shared library's text is copied into each process, not shared between
them.
.IP \(bu
.BR ps
reports that it cannot find a controlling terminal.
.IP \(bu
System V IPC,
.BR mount ,
.B statfs
and
.B ptrace
are not implemented.
.IP \(bu
Networking is the client's half of the STREAMS transport interface, as
.BR svr4 (4)
describes: a program can connect through
.I /dev/tcp
or, to the X server, through
.IR /dev/spx ,
and cannot listen.
INTERACTIVE UNIX's X clients reach a local display
.RB ( :0 )
and not a remote one, which they look up through a name server of their
own.
.IP \(bu
.I /dev
is substrate's own, and the 4.3BSD terminal requests
.RB ( TIOCGETP
and the rest) are carried out on any terminal, as
.BR svr4 (4)
describes;
.B xterm
runs a shell on a pseudo-terminal it finds as
.IR /dev/ptyp0 .
.SH VERIFIED
Against AT&T UNIX System V/386 Release 3.2.3 and INTERACTIVE UNIX 3.0, on
the i386 and x86_64 kernels: the statically linked
.I /bin/sh
with pipes, command substitution, redirection, traps and exit statuses;
.BR ksh ;
programs linked against
.I /shlib/libc_s
.RB ( ls ,
.BR cat ,
.BR date ,
.BR id ,
.BR uname ,
.BR sort ,
.BR wc ,
.BR grep ,
.BR sed ,
.BR expr ,
.BR awk ,
.BR file );
and each system's C compiler, which compiles and links a program that then
runs.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR svr4 (4),
.BR xenix (4),
.BR execve (2)
.SH HISTORY
A COFF loader that mapped sections from a file already in memory, and a
personality that was a table of native calls, existed from early on;
neither was reachable from
.BR execve (2).
The loader and personality described here date from October 2026.
